<<<
The writer knew that after was to be a preposition governing Mr B. &c. finding; but the reader takes it for a conjunction
===
The writer knew that *after* was to be a preposition governing *Mr B. &c. finding*; but the reader takes it for a conjunction
>>>

<<<
Such things happen with the Fusrp PARTICIPLE./Four years, the years that followed her marriage, suffice Lady Younghusband for her somewhat elaborate study, ‘ Marie Antoinette: Her Early Youth, 1770- 1774” (Macmillan & Co., 15s. net).
===
Such things happen with the ^^FUSED PARTICIPLE^^./*Four years, the years that followed her marriage, suffice Lady Younghusband for her somewhat elaborate study, 'Marie Antoinette: Her Early Youth, 1770-1774' (Macmillan & Co., 15s. net).*
>>>

<<<
The reader does not dream of jumping over Lady Y. to get at the owner of her (marriage) till 1770-1774 at the end throws a new light on the four years. See Pronouns for more such false scent.
===
The reader does not dream of jumping over Lady Y. to get at the owner of *her* (marriage) till *1770-1774* at the end throws a new light on the four years. See ^^PRONOUNS^^ for more such false scent.
>>>

<<<
/The official announcement at Rome that the Ottoman Government, having failed to meet Italy’s demands, Italy & Turkey were in a state of war from 2.30 yesterday afternoon, was promptly followed by hostilities.
===
/*The official announcement at Rome that the Ottoman Government, having failed to meet Italy's demands, Italy & Turkey were in a state of war from 2.30 yesterday afternoon, was promptly followed by hostilities.*
>>>

<<<
The punctuation (see ABSOLUTE CONSTRUCTION) deludes us into expecting a verb for the Ottoman Government, instead of which comes a new subject.
===
The punctuation (see ^^ABSOLUTE CONSTRUCTION^^) deludes us into expecting a verb for *the Ottoman Government*, instead of which comes a new subject.
>>>

